      "text": "To determine the mean of the set \\(\\{n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15\\}\\), we first need to find the value of \\(n\\). Given that the median of the set is 9, we can use the properties of the median in a set of data.\n\nThe median of a set of 5 numbers is the middle one when the numbers are arranged in increasing order. Since we know the median is 9, we can arrange the numbers in increasing order as follows:\n\n\\[n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15, n\\]\n\nIn either case, the five numbers are arranged in ascending order. The median will be the third number in this ordered list. Given that the median is 9, we can write:\n\n\\[n + 6 = 9\\]\n\nSolving for \\(n\\):\n\n\\[n = 9 - 6 = 3\\]\n\nNow we have the values of \\(n\\):\n\n\\[n = 3\\]\n\nThe set of numbers is \\(\\{3, 3 + 5, 3 + 6, 3 + 9, 3 + 15\\} = \\{3, 8, 11, 12, 18\\}\\).\n\nNext, we calculate the mean of this set. The mean is the sum of the numbers divided by the number of elements in the set. So we start by calculating the sum of the numbers:\n\n\\[3 + 8 + 11 + 12 + 18\\]\n\nWe can add these together step by step:\n\n\\[3 + 8 = 11\\]\n\\[11 + 11 = 22\\]\n\\[22 + 12 = 34\\]\n\\[34 + 18 = 52\\]\n\nThe sum of the numbers is 52. Since there are 5 numbers in the set, the mean is:\n\n\\[\\frac{52}{5} = 10.4\\]\n\nSo the mean of the set is 10.4. The final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{10.4}\\]\n\nHere is a Python code snippet to verify the solution.\n\n```python\n# Define the values of n\nn = 3\n# Define the set\nnumbers = [n, n + 5, n + 6, n + 9, n + 15]\n# Calculate the mean\nmean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\nprint(mean)\n```",
